1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,665
In the harbour at Baiae, a Roman pier has been
standing in salt water for two thousand years.

2
00:00:06,665 --> 00:00:08,600
It has never been repaired.

3
00:00:08,950 --> 00:00:16,825
A few hundred metres away, a concrete jetty
poured in 1985 is already crumbling into the sea.

4
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:23,147
For most of the twentieth century, engineers
assumed the Romans had simply been lucky.

5
00:00:23,147 --> 00:00:27,800
Their concrete was weaker than ours
by every measure that mattered.

6
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:33,900
It carried less load. It set more slowly.
On paper, it should have failed first.

7
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:39,935
The answer turned out to be hiding in the
one ingredient nobody thought to question.

8
00:00:39,935 --> 00:00:45,350
Roman builders mixed their mortar with
volcanic ash from the slopes of Vesuvius.

9
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:51,575
When seawater seeps into that mixture, it
does not corrode the structure. It reacts.

10
00:00:51,925 --> 00:00:56,517
New crystals grow inside the
cracks, knitting the fracture shut.

11
00:00:56,517 --> 00:01:00,750
The material gets stronger the
longer it sits in the ocean.

12
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:05,375
Modern concrete does the
opposite, and it does it fast.
